\section{Age values (age)}{

An \code{age} vector stores numerical ages with fractional precision and one
explicit time unit. Create it with \code{\link[=age]{age()}}, convert with \code{\link[=as_age]{as_age()}}, or
calculate it from dates with \code{\link[=calculate_age]{calculate_age()}}. Printing shows completed units
by default; \code{as.numeric()} retrieves stored fractions. See \code{\link[=age]{age()}} for display
options, conversions and supported operations.
}

\section{Interval definitions (age_interval)}{

An \code{age_interval} vector represents age ranges using the \code{ivs} interval
representation. Each range includes its start and excludes its end: \verb{[start, end)}. For example, \verb{[1, 5)} includes completed ages 1 through 4. A vector can
carry category names and one common unit. Use \code{\link[=age_interval]{age_interval()}} to define
ranges and \code{\link[=as_age_interval]{as_age_interval()}} to convert supported inputs. These ranges can
define categories or represent observations whose exact ages are unknown.
}

\section{Category assignments (age_group)}{

An \code{age_group} vector records one category assignment per observation as an
ordered factor and retains the interval definition for each available
category. \code{\link[=categorize_age]{categorize_age()}} creates it by classifying ages; \code{\link[=age_group]{age_group()}}
constructs it from existing labels and definitions. \code{\link[=group_intervals]{group_intervals()}}
retrieves those definitions, including unused categories. See \link[=age_group-class]{age_group class}
for the representation and supported operations.
}

\section{Reusable classification definitions (age_scheme)}{

An \code{age_scheme} contains named \code{age_interval} definitions with identity,
purpose, provenance and coverage information. It represents a reusable
classification, rather than observations or their assignments. Its domain
can be inferred from the interval span or specified explicitly; completeness
is calculated. Use \code{\link[=age_scheme]{age_scheme()}} to construct one and \code{\link[=scheme_intervals]{scheme_intervals()}}
to retrieve its interval definitions. Schemes and groups contain intervals;
neither inherits from the interval class.
}

\section{How the classes work together}{

Start with \code{age} values and define categories with an \code{age_interval} vector
or an \code{age_scheme}. Pass them to \code{\link[=categorize_age]{categorize_age()}} to obtain \code{age_group}
assignments. The result retains category definitions, but not original ages
or scheme provenance. Keep those inputs separately for later reclassification
or attribution. \code{\link[=classify_age_scheme]{classify_age_scheme()}} provides all memberships as a data
frame when categories can overlap.
}

